(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["heart-failure"]="<h1>Heart Failure Physiology</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-heart-failure\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> What is heart failure?</h2>\n<p><strong>Heart failure</strong> is when the heart <strong>can't pump enough blood for the body's needs</strong>, or can only do so <strong>at abnormally high filling pressures</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both parts matter. Some patients have a near-normal cardiac output at rest, but only because their filling pressures are very high, and that high pressure is what causes their symptoms. So heart failure is <strong>not simply &quot;low cardiac output&quot;</strong>.</p>\n<p>Every failing heart causes two kinds of problem:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Forward failure:</strong> too little blood goes <strong>out</strong>, so the organs are underperfused. This causes tiredness, poor exercise tolerance, and kidneys that hold on to salt and water.</li>\n<li><strong>Backward failure:</strong> blood <strong>backs up</strong> behind the failing chamber, so the veins upstream become congested. This causes fluid in the lungs or the body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are <strong>two sides of the same failing pump</strong>, not two different diseases.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-systolic-and-diastolic-failure-can-t-squeeze-or-can-t-fill\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Systolic and diastolic failure: can't squeeze, or can't fill?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Systolic failure</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Diastolic failure</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>The problem</strong></td>\n  <td>The ventricle can't <strong>squeeze</strong> properly</td>\n  <td>The ventricle can't <strong>relax and fill</strong> properly (it is stiff)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"ejection-fraction\">Ejection fraction</span></strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Reduced</strong> (it ejects a smaller fraction)</td>\n  <td>Often <strong>preserved</strong> (normal fraction of a small volume)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Filling pressure</strong></td>\n  <td>High, because blood is left behind after each beat</td>\n  <td>High, because a stiff ventricle needs high pressure to fill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Typical causes</strong></td>\n  <td>Heart attack, cardiomyopathy</td>\n  <td>Long-standing high blood pressure, ageing, hypertrophy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Both raise filling pressure, so both cause congestion.</strong> A normal ejection fraction does <strong>not</strong> rule out heart failure: a stiff ventricle can eject a normal percentage of a small, high-pressure fill. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>A fast heart makes both worse.</strong> Diastole shortens, and a stiff ventricle, which already fills slowly, gets even less time to fill. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output\">→ Cardiac Output, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Left versus right failure: where does the blood back up?</h2>\n<p><strong>Blood backs up into the circulation that feeds the failing ventricle.</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure hf-sides-figure\" id=\"cvs21-sides\"><figcaption>Figure 1. Left versus right failure: where the blood backs up</figcaption><div class=\"hf-sides\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Left and right heart failure chains\"><div class=\"hf-col hf-left\"><strong>Left ventricle fails</strong><span>Left atrial pressure ↑</span><span>Lung vein and capillary pressure ↑</span><span>Fluid filters into the lungs</span><span class=\"hf-end\">Breathlessness, worse lying flat (pulmonary oedema)</span></div><div class=\"hf-col hf-right\"><strong>Right ventricle fails</strong><span>Right atrial pressure ↑</span><span>Systemic vein pressure ↑</span><span>Congested liver and tissues</span><span class=\"hf-end\">Raised JVP, swollen liver, ascites, swollen ankles</span></div></div><p>Blood backs up into the circulation that feeds the failing ventricle. Left failure is the commonest cause of right failure.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Left heart failure → the lungs.</strong> The left ventricle can't empty, so pressure rises in the left atrium and the lung veins. The lung capillaries filter fluid into the lungs faster than the lymphatics can clear it. This is <strong><span data-term=\"pulmonary-oedema\">pulmonary oedema</span></strong>, and it causes <strong>breathlessness</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema\">→ Oedema, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why worse lying flat (orthopnoea)?</strong> Lying down, blood that pooled in the legs returns to the chest. The failing left ventricle can't pump the extra on, so lung pressure rises further. Patients prop themselves up on pillows to breathe.</p>\n<p><strong>Right heart failure → the body.</strong> The right ventricle can't empty, so pressure rises in the right atrium and all the systemic veins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Raised <span data-term=\"jvp\">JVP</span></strong>, the visible sign of high right atrial pressure <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses?section=7-which-abnormal-jvp-waves-are-most-important\">→ Pulses and JVP, §7</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Swollen, tender liver</strong>, congested with blood</li>\n<li><strong>Ascites</strong>: fluid in the abdomen</li>\n<li><strong>Swollen ankles</strong>: dependent, <span data-term=\"pitting\">pitting oedema</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The two often go together.</strong> The commonest cause of right failure is left failure: high lung pressure strains the right ventricle, which has to pump into it.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why can a patient have heart failure with a normal ejection fraction?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. A stiff ventricle ejects a normal fraction of a small volume at high pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A normal ejection fraction always means a normal cardiac output</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Ejection fraction is measured only in the right ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart compensates fully by beating more slowly</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"heart-failure:9f7b8859\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> In diastolic failure the ventricle fills poorly and needs high pressure. The fraction ejected is normal, but filling is not.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A patient with left ventricular failure becomes breathless. What is the mechanism?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Low blood pressure reduces oxygen delivery to the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Pressure backs up into the lung veins, so fluid leaks into the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The right ventricle pumps too much blood into the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Fluid in the abdomen pushes up on the diaphragm</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"heart-failure:b98bb730\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> High left atrial pressure raises lung capillary pressure, causing pulmonary oedema.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Which finding points to right-sided rather than left-sided failure?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Breathlessness when lying flat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Fluid in the lungs on a chest X-ray</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Breathlessness on exertion</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A raised JVP with swollen ankles</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"heart-failure:84478e6e\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Right failure raises systemic venous pressure: a raised JVP, liver congestion and ankle oedema. The others reflect lung congestion from left failure.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> What does the body do about it, and why does that make it worse?</h2>\n<p>When the heart pumps too little, the <strong>kidneys and <span data-term=\"baroreceptors\">baroreceptors</span> sense a low arterial pressure</strong> (the arteries are &quot;underfilled&quot;) and switch on three rescue systems:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>System</th>\n  <th>What it does</th>\n  <th>Short-term help</th>\n  <th>Long-term harm</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Sympathetic nerves</strong></td>\n  <td>Faster, stronger heart; tighter arterioles</td>\n  <td>Holds up blood pressure and output</td>\n  <td>Fast rate shortens filling; tight arterioles raise <strong><span data-term=\"afterload\">afterload</span></strong>; the heart muscle is overworked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Renin–angiotensin–<span data-term=\"aldosterone\">aldosterone</span> (RAAS)</strong></td>\n  <td>Constricts vessels; the kidneys keep salt and water</td>\n  <td>Raises blood volume and pressure</td>\n  <td>More volume → <strong>more congestion and oedema</strong>; higher afterload</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"adh\">ADH</span></strong></td>\n  <td>The kidneys keep water</td>\n  <td>Raises blood volume</td>\n  <td>Worsens fluid overload</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The vicious circle:</strong> the failing heart → the body senses &quot;too little blood&quot; → it keeps more fluid and tightens vessels → the heart faces more filling pressure and more afterload → it fails further.</p>\n<p><strong>The paradox:</strong> a patient can be <strong>waterlogged</strong> (oedema everywhere) while the kidneys still act as if the body is <strong>short of blood</strong>. The total body fluid is high, but the arteries are underfilled because the pump is weak. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Frank–Starling helps less and less.</strong> At first, extra filling stretches the ventricle and raises <span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">stroke volume</span>. But a failing ventricle sits on a <strong>flat, low curve</strong>, so extra filling mainly raises pressure and congestion. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=5-how-do-we-read-frank-starling-curves\">→ Cardiac Output, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Remodelling.</strong> Over months, the ventricle dilates or thickens and becomes less efficient: another long-term harm of chronic stimulation.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Section 4\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Section 4 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Section 4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why do the kidneys retain salt and water in heart failure, even when the patient is oedematous?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The kidneys are damaged directly by the failing heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They sense underfilled arteries from the weak pump and activate RAAS</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Oedema fluid blocks the kidneys' filtration</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The patient drinks more water because of thirst alone</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"heart-failure:54a061d7\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Low effective arterial filling triggers RAAS and ADH, so the kidneys keep fluid despite the total body excess.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How does sympathetic activation eventually harm the failing heart?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It slows the heart, so cardiac output falls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It dilates the arteries, so blood pressure falls too far</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It raises afterload and heart rate, increasing the heart's work</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It lowers blood volume through more urine production</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"heart-failure:e3d86ebb\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Tighter arterioles raise afterload, and a faster rate shortens filling and raises oxygen demand, so the weak heart works harder.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does giving extra fluid help a failing heart only a little?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Its Frank–Starling curve is low and flat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Extra fluid lowers the heart's contractility</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The extra fluid goes straight to the kidneys</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Extra fluid lowers the heart rate too much</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"heart-failure:c87dc6b7\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> On a flat curve, more filling adds little stroke volume but raises pressure and congestion.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"5-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Heart failure:</strong> the heart can't meet the body's needs, or only at high filling pressures. It is not just low output.</li>\n<li><strong>Forward failure</strong> = too little output. <strong>Backward failure</strong> = congestion upstream. They are two sides of one problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Systolic failure</strong> = can't squeeze (low EF). <strong>Diastolic failure</strong> = can't fill (stiff; EF often preserved). Both raise filling pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Left failure → lungs:</strong> pulmonary oedema, breathlessness, worse lying flat (orthopnoea).</li>\n<li><strong>Right failure → body:</strong> raised JVP, congested liver, ascites, ankle oedema. Left failure is its commonest cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Rescue systems</strong> (sympathetic, RAAS, ADH) hold up pressure at first, but raise afterload and fluid, so they worsen failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Paradox:</strong> waterlogged, yet the kidneys sense &quot;too little blood&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Frank–Starling</strong> gives diminishing returns on a failing, flat curve.</li>\n</ol>\n";
